
1. What std offers
--------------------------------------------------------------
std::process............................ exit, abort, Command
std::thread............................. park, unpark, sleep
a way to hear about SIGINT.............. none

There is no std::signal. Ctrl-C is therefore whatever the kernel
does by default: the process ends, no unwinding, no Drop, and no
destructor for the file you were half-way through writing.

2. Installing a handler through the C ABI
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal(SIGINT, on_sigint) returned...... 0
...which is SIG_DFL..................... true

SIG_DFL is 0 and SIG_IGN is 1; anything else is a function
pointer. That return value is the only way back to what was there,
and nothing keeps a copy for you.

3. A real SIGINT, delivered to ourselves
--------------------------------------------------------------
INTERRUPTED before...................... false
raise(SIGINT) returned.................. 0
INTERRUPTED after....................... true
the number the handler was passed....... 2

The handler ran, returned, and execution carried on at the next
line. A handler that returns resumes the code it interrupted: it is
a call, not an unwind, so nothing was dropped on the way through.

4. The flag is the program, not the handler
--------------------------------------------------------------
round 1................................. working
round 2................................. working
round 3................................. saw the flag, shutting down

This is the whole Rust idiom. The handler sets an atomic; the work
happens back in ordinary code, where allocating, locking and
printing are allowed again. Everything a Python handler may do
directly, a Rust handler has to ask for by leaving a note.

5. What the handler may not do
--------------------------------------------------------------
It is a C signal handler, so the rule is C's rule: only
async-signal-safe calls. That list is short, and nothing which
locks or allocates is on it.

    println!(...)....................... takes the Stdout lock
    String::from, vec![], format!....... allocates
    Mutex::lock......................... locks
    an atomic store..................... fine, and is all this one does

The failure is a deadlock, not a compile error: the signal arrives
while main holds the Stdout lock, the handler asks for the same
lock on the same thread, and the process stops there. Nothing in
the type system says a fn is a signal handler, so nothing checks it.

6. Ignoring, and putting the default back
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN) returned........ a function pointer
...neither SIG_DFL nor SIG_IGN.......... true
handler calls before a raise............ 2
handler calls after it.................. 2

SIG_IGN is the kernel discarding the signal before anyone is woken.

signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL) returned........ 1, which is SIG_IGN

SIGINT is back to ending the process. Raising it now would stop this
program with no further output, which is why this line is the last.
